Description:

Mailbox sharing over JMAP can now be done the way JMAP Mail Sharing
(draft-ietf-jmap-mail-sharing) says to, with the
urn:ietf:params:jmap:mail:share capability.  It lives with the other
non-standard extensions for now, because the draft hasn't been published yet.

Under that capability, the values in shareWith are MailboxRights objects, the
same shape as myRights, so each right can be granted on its own instead of in
one of the three bundles our own extension offers.  MailboxRights also gains
mayShare, the right to modify shareWith, which is the IMAP "a" right by itself.
That's not the same as our mayAdmin, which also requires "x" and "k", so the
two can disagree: a sharee given only "a" has mayShare but not mayAdmin.  A
client that doesn't use mail:share doesn't see mayShare at all.

mayRename can be read but not written.  It isn't a right we store anywhere --
it's derived from mayDelete on the mailbox and mayCreateChild on the parent --
so a client may send back the value we gave it, which is what a
read-modify-write of shareWith does, but asking us to change it is forbidden.

Our own https://cyrusimap.org/ns/jmap/mail spelling of shareWith still works,
and will until clients have moved over.  Each request gets exactly the
vocabulary it asked for, and the two can't be mixed: mayRead, mayWrite and
mayAdmin are rejected as unknown rights under mail:share, and the MailboxRights
rights are rejected as unknown without it.

While both vocabularies are in use, be aware that the old one can't express
everything the new one can, and that the view through it is lossy.  A sharee
granted, say, only maySetSeen appears to have none of mayRead, mayWrite or
mayAdmin, so an old client doing a read-modify-write of shareWith will take
that grant away.  This will stop being a concern once the old spelling is gone.


